  "82834711-4ea2-43cc-a498-d4f79e24c412": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p16-gradient-definition",
     "replacement": "Consider two lines with gradients $m_1$ and $m_2$.\n\n**Parallel** lines have the **same gradient**: $m_1=m_2$.\n\n**Perpendicular** lines have gradients that **multiply to** $-1$: $m_1m_2=-1$. A horizontal line and a vertical line are also perpendicular.",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p16-gradient-definition",
     "targetFingerprint": "a09cd9616e27555c3d891c440664f2d5f1b944e5545e4e124d07bb205bafb5e6",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
   "run": ".booklet-work/pedagogy-audit-v2/s6adv-working-with-functions-4-linear-functions-v1/20261111-090558",
   "revision": 17
  },
  "c14e56c0-98f4-40de-a9fb-0e878075e882": {
   "kind": "theory-definition",
   "payloads": [
    {
     "op": "theory",
     "blockId": "p17-theory",
     "replacement": "**Gradient of a parallel line**\n\n$$m_{\\mathrm{par}}=m_{\\mathrm{original}}$$\n**Gradient of a perpendicular line** (original line neither horizontal nor vertical)\n\n$$m_{\\mathrm{perp}}=-\\frac{1}{m_{\\mathrm{original}}}$$\n\n1. Determine gradient $m_{\\mathrm{new}}$ of the parallel/perpendicular line.\n2. Write the equation $y-y_1=m_{\\mathrm{new}}(x-x_1)$\n3. Substitute new gradient and given point into the formula.\n4. Rearrange to $y=mx+c$",
     "autoApply": true,
     "key": "theory|p17-theory",
     "targetFingerprint": "450e07f3b06968c70fee7137feed96577d8d026890767f32dd6610e29cf73bd6",
     "applied": true
    }
   ],
